module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.ReductiveBorelPairStructure
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.RankOneRootInterfaces
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Lattice
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.ReductiveRankOneReflections
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.ReductiveRootSubgroupParametrizations
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.BorelRootProductCoordinates
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.BorelSchemeNormalizer
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.ReductiveTorusSelfCentralizer

/-! # The normalizer quotient realizes the rational root reflection group

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 21.37 and Corollary 21.38,
pp.435--436; Appendix C.15 and C.46, pp.611, 618--619.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-weyl-reflection-realization)

The root-span parameter is fixed by equality with its actual rational span.
The full rational action and its restriction are characterized by their values.
Reflections are characterized on the spanning root vectors, so no choice of
an abstract representation or a positive definite form is part of the statement.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory MonoidalCategory CartesianMonoidalCategory
open scoped MonObj

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The normalizer quotient of a split maximal torus acts faithfully on the rational
root span, with image exactly the group generated by the coroot reflections.
Source: Milne, Theorem 21.37 and Corollary 21.38, pp.435--436;
Appendix C.15 and C.46, pp.611, 618--619.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-weyl-reflection-realization)

Proof plan:
1. Use lem-sfb-reductive-rank-one-reflections for finiteness and the normalizer
   action on the torus and characters. Replacing n by nt does not change the
   character action, since T is commutative. Substitution gives the group law.
   Extend to rational tensors and restrict to the invariant span of the roots.
2. Conjugation carries each root tangent space to that of the transformed root.
   Uniqueness in lem-sfb-reductive-root-subgroup-parametrizations identifies the
   conjugate root subgroups. By lem-sfb-borel-root-product-coordinates and the
   radical splitting in lem-sfb-reductive-borel-pair-structure, these positive root
   subgroups together with T generate B as a subgroup scheme. Thus a normalizer
   element preserving positive roots normalizes B scheme-theoretically.
3. By lem-sfb-borel-scheme-normalizer that element lies in B. The projection B → T
   from lem-sfb-reductive-borel-pair-structure shows that it commutes with T on
   every algebra: apply the projection to ntn⁻¹ and use commutativity of T.
   By lem-sfb-reductive-torus-self-centralizer it lies in T. The positive-root
   stabilizer in the quotient is therefore trivial, proving faithfulness on E.
4. Extend E to the reals. Average a positive definite form over the finite quotient.
   Rank-one reflections and invariance identify the coroot formula with orthogonal
   reflection. The root datum and its base give the finite spanning reduced
   crystallographic real root system. If a second coroot gives the same root
   reflection direction, the product is 1 + N with N² = 0 and finite order;
   characteristic zero forces N = 0. Thus the specified coroots give these maps.
5. Apply lem-sfb-root-chamber-simple-transitivity. The reflection group carries
   the positive chamber to any normalizer translate. Lift the corresponding word
   in reflections using the rank-one representatives. The quotient of the original
   element by this word preserves the chamber, hence the positive roots, and step 3
   makes it trivial. This proves equality of images; real scalar extension detects
   equality of rational maps, giving precisely the stated rational reflection group.
6. For no roots, E = 0 and every element preserves the empty positive set.
   Step 3 makes the quotient trivial; the closure of the empty reflection family
   is the trivial subgroup. This includes tori and the rank-zero group.
-/
theorem weyl_reflection_realization
    {F : Type u} [Field F] [IsAlgClosed F]
    (G T B : Grp (Over (Spec (.of F))))
    (hG : ReductiveTorusCentralizer.IsReductive G)
    (iT : T ⟶ G) (hT : ReductiveTorusCentralizer.IsMaximalTorus iT)
    (hsplit : ∃ r : ℕ, Nonempty (T ≅ MultiplicativeType.splitTorus F r))
    (iB : B ⟶ G) (hB : IsBorelGroupScheme iB.hom.hom)
    (j : T ⟶ B) (hj : j ≫ iB = iT)
    [IsCommMonObj T.X] [IsCommMonObj (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)]
    (aG : ModObj T.X G.X) (aB : ModObj T.X B.X)
    (Φ : Finset (Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F))))
    (P : RootDatum Φ
      (Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)))
      (Additive (Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F) ⟶ T)))
    (b : P.Base) [P.IsReduced]
    (hconjG : aG.smul = (iT.hom.hom ⊗ₘ 𝟙 G.X) ≫ GrpObj.conj G.X)
    (hconjB : aB.smul = (j.hom.hom ⊗ₘ 𝟙 B.X) ≫ GrpObj.conj B.X)
    (hroot : ∀ α : Φ, P.root α = α.val)
    (hpair : ∀ (χ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)))
      (ν : Additive (Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F) ⟶ T)),
      ν.toMul.hom.hom ≫ χ.toMul.hom.hom =
        (𝟙 (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)) ^ (P.toLinearMap χ ν))
    (hweights : ∀ χ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)),
      χ ∈ Φ ↔ χ ≠ 0 ∧ @TorusCharacterTangent.weightSpace F _ T G aG χ.toMul ≠ ⊥)
    (hpos : ∀ α : Φ, b.IsPos α ↔
      @TorusCharacterTangent.weightSpace F _ T B aB α.val.toMul ≠ ⊥)
    (E : Submodule ℚ (TensorProduct ℤ ℚ
      (Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)))))
    (hE : E = Submodule.span ℚ
      (Set.range (fun α : Φ ↦ TensorProduct.tmul ℤ (1 : ℚ) (P.root α)))) :
    Finite (RankOneRoots.Weyl iT) ∧
    ∃ (act : RankOneRoots.Weyl iT →* Aut T)
      (ract : RankOneRoots.Weyl iT →*
        (TensorProduct ℤ ℚ
          (Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F))) ≃ₗ[ℚ]
         TensorProduct ℤ ℚ
          (Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)))))
      (r : RankOneRoots.Weyl iT →* (E ≃ₗ[ℚ] E))
      (root : Φ → E) (s : Φ → (E ≃ₗ[ℚ] E)),
      (∀ (n : RankOneRoots.normalizer iT) (A : CommAlgCat.{u} F)
        (t : StandardGroupSchemes.affine F A ⟶ T.X),
        t ≫ (act (QuotientGroup.mk n)).hom.hom.hom ≫ iT.hom.hom =
          (toUnit (StandardGroupSchemes.affine F A) ≫ n.val) * (t ≫ iT.hom.hom) *
            (toUnit (StandardGroupSchemes.affine F A) ≫ n.val)⁻¹) ∧
      (∀ (w : RankOneRoots.Weyl iT) (q : ℚ)
        (χ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F))),
        ract w (TensorProduct.tmul ℤ q χ) =
          TensorProduct.tmul ℤ q (Additive.ofMul ((act w).inv ≫ χ.toMul))) ∧
      (∀ (w : RankOneRoots.Weyl iT) (x : E), (r w x).val = ract w x.val) ∧
      Function.Injective r ∧
      (∀ α : Φ, (root α).val = TensorProduct.tmul ℤ (1 : ℚ) (P.root α)) ∧
      (∀ α β : Φ,
        s α (root β) = root β - (P.toLinearMap (P.root β) (P.coroot α) : ℚ) • root α) ∧
      r.range = Subgroup.closure (Set.range s) ∧
      (∀ w : RankOneRoots.Weyl iT,
        Set.image (fun χ : Additive (T ⟶ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative F)) ↦
          Additive.ofMul ((act w).inv ≫ χ.toMul)) (Φ : Set _) = (Φ : Set _)) ∧
      (Φ = ∅ → E = ⊥ ∧ (∀ w : RankOneRoots.Weyl iT, w = 1) ∧
        Subgroup.closure (Set.range s) = ⊥) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
